# Lime-Bit Project Context

Last updated: 2026-09-18

## Project context workflow

Treat this `.mdl` file as the authoritative project context for future prompts.
Read it before changing architecture, engine integration, or visual structure.
Keep it current when behavior, dependencies, UI contracts, or validation status
changes; record verified facts rather than assumptions.

## Product intent

Lime-Bit is intended to become a real desktop download/torrent client in Rust.
It must not contain demo torrents, fake progress, hard-coded Ubuntu actions, or
mock engine state. The UI should preserve the current dark green/lime visual
language: approximately 55% alpha translucent surfaces and lime outlines.

## Stack

- Rust 2021 binary crate: `lime-bit`
- Async runtime: Tokio 1 with full features
- Engine: `gosh-dl` from the upstream GitHub `main` branch (currently package
  version `0.6.3`; Cargo default features: `http`, `torrent`, `storage`)
- UI: `gpui-kit 0.6.1` plus direct `gpui-component 0.6.1` for `Input` and
  `InputState`; `rfd 0.15.4` for an extension-filtered native file picker
- Serialization: serde and serde_json
- Errors/logging: anyhow, log, env_logger

## Files

- `src/main.rs`: starts the Tokio engine thread, opens the GPUI window, routes
  commands and periodic status snapshots.
- `src/app.rs`: LimeBitApp GPUI view, URI input, notices, torrent list, theme.
- `src/state.rs`: UI-to-engine commands and engine-to-UI events.
- `src/torrent.rs`: thin wrapper around `Arc<gosh_dl::DownloadEngine>`;
  translates gosh-dl statuses into UI data and retains a session ID lookup map.
- `src/ui/torrent_row.rs`: status row, progress bar, transfer rates, peer/
  seeder/connection/ETA telemetry, and action buttons.
- `CHANGES.md`: user-facing unreleased change log.
- `src/ui/mod.rs`: UI module declarations.
- `cargo.toml`: dependency manifest; `Cargo.lock` is generated by Cargo.

## Current engine behavior

`main.rs` initializes `tracing-subscriber` using `RUST_LOG` when present and
routes formatted tracing records into the optional in-app backend console. On
Windows the binary uses the GUI subsystem, so no separate command prompt is
shown.
`main.rs` enables `gosh-dl 0.6.3`'s documented BEP 29 uTP transport with
`PreferUtp` and TCP fallback, so UDP-capable peers use uTP while incompatible
peers remain reachable over TCP. The tracker client automatically supports
BEP 15 UDP tracker announces. BitTorrent remains the default peer protocol;
TCP is retained for compatibility, uTP is preferred when available, DHT is
enabled for trackerless discovery, PEX is enabled for peer discovery, LPD is
available for local discovery, and magnet links are supported as torrent
identifiers. The app creates the parent directory for
`config.get_database_path()`, assigns it to `config.database_path`, and starts
`DownloadEngine::new(config)`. This enables gosh-dl SQLite persistence rather
than an in-memory-only session.

The engine loop polls `DownloadEngine::list()` every 500ms and sends real
statuses to GPUI. GPUI's `on_window_closed` callback sends a `Shutdown` command;
the engine awaits `DownloadEngine::shutdown()` and the main thread joins the
engine thread before process exit, so active torrents are persisted/stopped
rather than being abandoned. At startup, persisted torrents remain paused until
explicit Resume. Commands are processed on the same Tokio engine thread.
Cancellation is deliberately different: the row is removed immediately, then
`DownloadEngine::cancel` runs in a spawned Tokio task, so a slow torrent stop
cannot block status polling or the rest of the UI command loop. The wrapper
waits for gosh-dl's own bounded stop operation instead of racing it with a
second five-second timeout. Torrent rows expose connected peers, seeders, and
ETA in addition to progress and transfer rates.

Current accepted add input:

- `magnet:` URI -> `DownloadEngine::add_magnet`
- `http://` or `https://` -> `DownloadEngine::add_http`
- local path ending in `.torrent` or `file://...` -> async file read followed by
  `DownloadEngine::add_torrent`

Torrent storage defaults to the configured Downloads directory. Single-file
`.torrent` inputs are placed in a sanitized folder named after the torrent;
multi-file torrents use gosh-dl's native `Downloads/torrent-name/...` layout.
OPEN LOCATION targets that torrent folder.

Current command support:

- Add with `uri` plus typed `DownloadOptions`
- Pause
- Resume
- Cancel with `delete_files` flag
- Verify
- Repair
- PauseAll
- ResumeAll
- CancelAll with `delete_files` flag; `CLEAR CACHE · KEEP FILES` uses
  `delete_files: false` to remove torrent/session metadata while preserving
  downloaded files, and `DELETE DATA` uses `delete_files: true` destructively

Current UI actions per row: Pause, Resume, Verify, Repair, Open Location, Remove.
`Open Location` uses the persisted save directory/filename path and launches the
platform file browser. Verify checks existing pieces against torrent hashes and
reports the verification result; Repair re-downloads damaged or missing data.
Remove
passes `delete_files: false`, removes the row immediately, and reports the
background cleanup result. A later delete-files confirmation action should
expose true explicitly.

## gosh-dl 0.6.3 API/capabilities verified from docs/source

The crate's default feature set is `[http, torrent, storage]`; optional
features include `full`, `metalink`, `recursive-http`, and `io-uring`.

Engine construction and persistence:

- `DownloadEngine::new(EngineConfig) -> Result<Arc<DownloadEngine>>`
- `DownloadEngine::with_storage(EngineConfig, Arc<dyn Storage>)`
- `EngineConfig` includes download directory, max concurrent downloads,
  max connections, segment size, global upload/download limits, scheduler
  rules, user agent, DHT, PEX, LPD, max peers, seed ratio, database path,
  HTTP config, and torrent config.
- `engine.subscribe() -> broadcast::Receiver<DownloadEvent>`
- `engine.shutdown()`

Add APIs:

- `add_http(url, DownloadOptions)`
- `add_magnet(magnet_uri, DownloadOptions)`
- `add_torrent(torrent_data, DownloadOptions)`
- With optional `recursive-http`: `discover_http_recursive` and
  `add_http_recursive`.
- With optional `metalink`: Metalink support should be integrated only after
  enabling the feature and checking its exact API.

- `DownloadOptions` fields:

- `start_paused`
- `priority`
- `save_dir`, `filename`
- `user_agent`, `referer`, `headers`, `cookies`
- `checksum`, `mirrors`
- `max_connections`
- `max_download_speed`, `max_upload_speed`
- `seed_ratio`
- `selected_files`
- `sequential`

`DownloadOptions` has builders such as `save_dir`, `filename`, `header`,
`checksum`, `mirror`, `max_connections`, speed-limit builders, `seed_ratio`,
`selected_files`, and `sequential`.

Control/status APIs:

- `status`, `list`, `active`, `waiting`, `stopped`
- `pause`, `resume`, `cancel`
- `pause_all`, `resume_all`, `cancel_all`
- `set_priority`, `get_priority`
- `global_stats`
- `get_bandwidth_limits`, `set_schedule_rules`, `get_schedule_rules`
- `verify`, `repair`
- `open_reader(id, offset)` for streaming bytes from an in-progress download

Status data available for GUI:

- `DownloadStatus`: ID, kind, state, priority, progress, metadata, optional
  torrent info, peers, creation/completion timestamps.
- `DownloadProgress`: total/completed bytes, download/upload speed, connections,
  seeders, peers, ETA.
- `DownloadState`: Queued, Connecting, Downloading, Seeding, Paused,
  Completed, Error.
- `DownloadEvent`: Added, Started, Progress, StateChanged, Completed, Failed,
  Removed, Paused, Resumed.

## Known design constraints

- `DownloadId::to_gid()` is intentionally lossy. Never reconstruct IDs with
  `DownloadId::from_gid()` for commands. Keep a map from the displayed GID to
  the original typed `DownloadId`; repopulate it from `engine.list()` after
  startup.
- The current 500ms list polling is functional but should eventually be
  replaced or supplemented by `engine.subscribe()` for event-driven updates.
- Startup recovery resumes persisted Paused records because gosh-dl 0.6.3
  does not distinguish user-paused records from interrupted active records
  after persistence restore; users can pause them again after startup.
- A torrent can legitimately show peers with zero throughput when peers choke
  the client, advertise no needed pieces, or the swarm has no usable seed.
  uTP is enabled with TCP fallback; `tracing-subscriber` is now enabled, so
  use `RUST_LOG=gosh_dl=debug` to inspect transport selection, handshakes,
  bitfields, unchokes, and block requests.
- `gosh-dl` 0.6.3 requires Rust 1.85 according to Cargo metadata.
- The `OPEN .TORRENT` button uses `rfd`'s native picker with a visible
  `BitTorrent metainfo (*.torrent)` file-type filter. Typed paths remain
  supported as a fallback.
- The root GPUI surface accepts operating-system file drops. Only files with a
  `.torrent` extension (case-insensitive) are submitted; dropped non-torrent
  files are ignored.
The current UI exposes the following `DownloadOptions` fields in a collapsible
  GUI options drawer: save directory, filename, user agent, referer, headers,
  cookies, mirrors, max connections, max download/upload speeds, seed ratio,
  selected file indices, priority, start-paused, and sequential mode. Values are
  parsed in `app.rs` and sent as a real `gosh_dl::DownloadOptions` in the Add
  command. Priority/start-paused/sequential use GPUI component dropdowns; numeric,
  path, HTTP, auth, mirror, and file-selection values remain labeled inputs. The
  component theme is explicitly switched to dark so these fields render against
  the Lime-Bit surface instead of the default white theme.

  The current UI exposes a live aggregate status bar with active/queued counts and
  aggregate upload/download rates, plus engine-backed Pause All, Resume All,
  Clear Cache · Keep Files, and destructive Delete Data controls. The backend console is rendered below the torrent list, spans the shell width,
  retains up to 2,000 tracing lines, and follows the resizable window layout.
  Settings apply global upload/download limits immediately; blank limits mean
  unlimited. Seed-after-completion can be disabled, which sends a zero seed
  ratio to gosh-dl. Periodic reconciliation now sends a `TorrentSnapshot`, so
  removed downloads disappear from the UI. A separate `CONSOLE` toggle displays
  recent backend tracing records without replacing or limiting the speed toolbar
  or torrent rows; up to 500 recent log lines are retained. Its COPY button
  writes the visible log buffer to the system clipboard. The default
  `gosh_dl=debug` filter exposes peer and piece-request diagnostics.

  All interactive controls now use `gpui_component::button::Button` from the
  GPUI component layer, with component variants (`success`, `warning`, `danger`,
  `info`, `secondary`), compact sizing, outlines, and `on_click` handlers. The
  translucent/lime layout remains custom styling around those accessible controls;
  clickable `div` button helpers were removed.

The GPUI window uses `gpui_component::TitleBar::window_options()` for a
client-decorated surface and renders an in-app Zed-style titlebar with explicit visible `—`, `⛶`, and `×`
minimize, maximize, and close controls. This hides the native Windows titlebar and uses direct GPUI
window actions for the three controls; the titlebar is intentionally kept on
the stable client-rendered path rather than using GPUI window-control hitboxes.
The content uses a compact density: 34px titlebar, 32px console, 8-unit
inputs, and consistent 4-unit outer padding.

## UI contract

Keep these existing theme constants/behavior unless the user requests a visual
change:

- Lime: `0xb8ff3c`
- Main translucent panel: `0x101a12b8`
- Surface: `0x17251bb8`
- Text: `0xecffe1`
- Muted: `0xa6bd9c`
- Root and rows use lime borders; surfaces use 8-bit alpha around `0xb8`,
  which is approximately 55% opacity.

Build onto the current custom titlebar, input row, notice area, and torrent
rows. Keep the compact scaling and do not replace the visual language with a
generic template.

## Planned implementation order

1. Add a real refresh/status command and a graceful application shutdown hook.
2. Switch status delivery to `engine.subscribe()` while retaining the periodic
   `TorrentSnapshot` reconciliation for crash/restart consistency.
3. Add priority editing and per-download file selection after torrent metadata
   is available; current selected-file indices are entered before add.
4. Add explicit delete-files confirmation rather than only the current safe
   remove action (`delete_files: false`).
5. Add optional `recursive-http` and `metalink` features only after checking
   their exact APIs and adding dedicated GUI flows; do not claim these are
   currently enabled.
6. Add streaming UI around `open_reader` if a player/preview feature is wanted.
7. Add tests for URI classification, option translation, ID-map behavior,
   status conversion, and command/event handling.

## Validation history

- `cargo check` passed after upgrading to `gosh-dl 0.6.3`.
- `cargo fmt` passed.
- `cargo test` passed after the options drawer, global controls, telemetry,
  dropdowns, and cancellation changes; the application currently has zero
  crate-local tests.
- `CHANGES.md` records the user-facing changes and the zero-throughput swarm
  limitation.
- The project is currently pinned to and compiling against `gosh-dl 0.6.3`.

## Honesty rule

Do not claim all gosh-dl features are exposed in the GUI until the options,
controls, and tests exist. The engine dependency supports more than the current
front end; this file tracks that gap explicitly.
